<Base
  title="Toolkit — Stevie Thomas"
  description="The tools, languages, and habits behind the projects. Mostly Python, a lot of plumbing, and a strong preference for things I can read."
>
  <section style="padding: clamp(2.5rem, 7vw, 4.5rem) 0 0;">
    <p class="eyebrow reveal" style="--i:0">Toolkit</p>
    <h1 class="reveal" style="--i:1">What I build with</h1>

    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        None of this is exotic. The interesting part of the work is almost never the tool — it is
        knowing what the number means. But people ask, so here it is.
      </p>

      <h3>The core</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Python</strong> for essentially everything. pandas and numpy for the data,
          scikit-learn when a model earns its place, scipy for the statistics. If a project needs
          something more exotic than that, my first suspicion is that I have overcomplicated it.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>SQL</strong> more than I expected. Most analysis time is spent getting the right
          rows out, not doing anything clever with them once they arrive.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>MuJoCo</strong> for the physics work — forward dynamics, torque-driven models,
          anything where I want to ask what force produced a motion rather than describe the motion.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>ffmpeg</strong> for all video. Every coaching clip, comparison edit, and overlay on
          this site went through it. It is the single highest-leverage tool I have learned that is
          not a language.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h3>Habits that have paid for themselves</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Fail loudly.</strong> No silent fallbacks, no bare exception handlers. If something
          is wrong I want a stack trace immediately, not a plausible number three steps downstream. A
          caught exception that returns a default is a bug that has been given permission to travel.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Less code.</strong> A helper function that exists to be called twice is usually
          worse than the two lines it replaced. Abstraction has a carrying cost and it gets paid by
          whoever reads it next, who is me.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Write the spec first.</strong> Every project that went well has a document written
          before the code that says what question it answers and what would count as an answer.
          Every project that wandered does not.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Cache the fetch, never the conclusion.</strong> Pulled data gets saved so I am not
          hammering someone's API. Derived results get regenerated every time, because a stale
          conclusion is indistinguishable from a fresh one until it embarrasses you.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Write down the traps.</strong> Every project has a file of things that cost me an
          hour — the null first frame, the frame rate that lies, the path separator that breaks a
          parser. It is the most reread document in every repo.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h3>This site</h3>
      <p>
        Astro, deployed by GitHub Actions to GitHub Pages. Content is markdown files in a folder;
        adding a page means writing one and pushing. No CMS, no database, no build step I cannot
        explain. Type is self-hosted rather than pulled from a font CDN, which is both faster and
        nobody else's business.
      </p>
      <p>
        Deliberately not branded like my employer. This is a personal site and it should look like a
        person made it.
      </p>

      <h3>What I avoid</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>
          Dashboards as a substitute for understanding. A tile is an answer to a question somebody
          else chose.
        </li>
        <li>
          Models I cannot explain to a hitter. If the only account of why the output is what it is
          involves the word "embedding", it is not going to change how anyone swings.
        </li>
        <li>
          Anything that requires the athlete data to leave the building. Which rules out a lot of
          otherwise convenient tools, and is not negotiable.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
